<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dutch Reformed Church</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1571">1571</a>–<a href="/years/2004">2004</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Nederlandse Hervormde Kerk</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> Reformed Church was the main Calvinist church of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and the established church of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>. It followed Dutch trade and settlement overseas, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. At the Cape its tradition shaped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaner</a> churches, and in 2004 it merged into the Protestant Church in the Netherlands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dutch-reformed-church.jpg" alt="Dutch Reformed Church" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zairon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amsterdam_Oude_Kerk_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Dutch Reformed Church</b> was the public church, but Catholics, Mennonites, Lutherans and Jews could worship privately, and Sephardic Jews from Portugal settled in Amsterdam.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Dutch was the official language and the <b>Dutch Reformed Church</b> the centre of community life, while Grey College, founded in Bloemfontein in 1855, and a growing number of state schools built a modest educational system.</blockquote><a href="/empire/orange-free-state">Orange Free State · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Calvinism was the state religion, and the <b>Dutch Reformed Church</b> the “public” or “privileged” church: anyone who wished to hold public office had to conform to it and take an oath.</blockquote><a href="/societies/dutch-republic">Social Hierarchy of the Dutch Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>The small republic had its own Volksraad and a church tradition closer to the <b>Dutch Reformed</b> establishment of the Cape.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-lydenburg">Republic of Lydenburg</a></li><li><blockquote>Its Lower Town was lined with warehouses, and the island had a <b>Dutch Reformed</b> church, a Jewish congregation and a polyglot merchant class.</blockquote><a href="/state/sint-eustatius">Sint Eustatius</a></li></ul></section>
